# register_bounds: reads and writes beyond the end of a register.
#
# Our own vectors, for the closed behavior of docs/arch-supports.md and
# impl/python/p4blo/arch/externs/register.py: a read at or beyond `size` yields zero
# and a write there is ignored. The register has 4 cells, bit<8> each, and
# the file replays on a fresh load, so r is all zero at the first packet
# and the state carries across every packet below.
#
# Each packet is one h_t: idx, val, got. Per packet, with i = idx:
#
#   x = r[i];  r[i] = x + val;  got = r[i]
#
# so in range the output's third byte is the cell's new value, and beyond
# the end it is what a read there returns, zero, whatever was written.
# The input's third byte is overwritten; it is 0xff in some packets to
# show that.
#
# Packet 1, idx 0, val 0x05:   r[0] = 0x00 -> 0x05;  got = 0x05
# Packet 2, idx 3, val 0x10:   the last cell: r[3] = 0x00 -> 0x10;  got = 0x10
# Packet 3, idx 4, val 0x07:   one past the end: read 0, the write is
#                              ignored, read 0 again;  got = 0x00
# Packet 4, idx 4, val 0x07:   again: nothing was stored anywhere, so the
#                              same;  got = 0x00
# Packet 5, idx 255, val 0x01: the largest index a packet can name, far
#                              past the end;  got = 0x00
# Packet 6, idx 0, val 0x03:   r[0] = 0x05, untouched by the packets in
#                              between: 0x05 + 0x03 = 0x08;  got = 0x08
# Packet 7, idx 3, val 0xef:   r[3] = 0x10 + 0xef = 0xff;  got = 0xff
# Packet 8, idx 3, val 0x01:   0xff + 0x01 wraps in bit<8> to 0x00;
#                              got = 0x00, a zero from arithmetic, not
#                              from the bound
# Packet 9, idx 3, val 0x02:   r[3] = 0x00 -> 0x02, so the wrap stored a
#                              zero where packets 3 to 5 stored nothing;
#                              got = 0x02
#
# The program declares no egress_port, so every packet leaves on port 0;
# `$` demands the exact length, the three header bytes and nothing else.

packet 0 00 05 ff
expect 0 00 05 05 $

packet 0 03 10 00
expect 0 03 10 10 $

packet 0 04 07 ff
expect 0 04 07 00 $

packet 0 04 07 00
expect 0 04 07 00 $

packet 0 ff 01 ff
expect 0 ff 01 00 $

packet 0 00 03 00
expect 0 00 03 08 $

packet 0 03 ef 00
expect 0 03 ef ff $

packet 0 03 01 00
expect 0 03 01 00 $

packet 0 03 02 00
expect 0 03 02 02 $
